From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Idea of Free Speech is Slipping Away

Tony Hamilton writes:

> Tim May:
> > > We'll have to create our own libertarian and extropian future. Just as
> > > we expected.
> 
> Isn't this the problem libertarians have always had - spending a great deal
> of effort attempting to preach their concepts to the masses, using
> traditional means to try to change the world? I certainly do hope extropians
> expected it would require more than this.

Tony joined the list after I went on vacation, so he is probably
unaware of my longstanding support for the "direct action" approach to
increasing my liberty. (I have not yet gone fully down the Duncan
Frissell route of hiding my assets, avoiding credit cards, and the
like--I'm not trying to fully characterize Duncan's position here--as
I'm not (yet) directly worried about them coming for me. More on this
later.)

I don't spend much time at all trying to preach to the masses. I find
the local Libertarian Party meeting stultifyingly boring. When the
founder of the LP, Dave Nolan, was in town I mentioned this to him,
and pointed out the futility: people don't really _want_ more
liberty...they want to have things without paying for them. And the LP
is a political joke: in the 20+ years of its existence, the LP has had
the chance to elect someone in at least 4700 Congressional elections (435 House
members x 10 elections + 100 Senate members x 3.5 elections)....and
has failed to elect even a single member, or even come close!

LP election returns peaked in 1980 at several percent, with a few local
elections hitting upwards of 10% (for example, in Silicon Valley).
Since then. it's been downhill, with LP returns now down in the
noise, so to speak. Face it, the LP approach is dead as a doornail.

I urge all capitalists, free marketeers, free speech advocates, and
lovers of liberty to put their efforts into other areas.

One area I am involved with, and which I have written about
extensively on this list, is the so-called Cypherpunks movement. I
won't go into it again, at least not now. I wrote a long essay,
"Libertaria in Cyberspace," a year or so ago, and then I reposted it
as a "golden oldie" some months back. (An archive site for such
postings is long overdue. Harry tells me one is coming.)

Strong crypto (encryption, privacy, digital money, anonymous mailers,
data havens, etc.) is the most powerful tool I can imagine. It will
ultimately break the back of government, will make taxation almost
impossible, will halt the suppression of free speech, and will make
national boundaries a thing of the past. And more, too.


> So, I'm all for putting some of these principles to the test. THis means
> proving that life extension pursuits are fruitful, that exploration and
> exploitation of space and its resources is convenient and economical, and
> that transhumanists are ultimately more likely to succeed in life. It would
> also be helpful to introduce a few world-changing technologies again, much
> in the way that the automobile and airplane changed the world, or the
> telephone, or even the computer. We haven't had many of these lately, and
> without any of these things becoming successful enough to affect the

It is my contention that strong crypto is precisely this--a technology
and set of ideas that will revolutionize the world.

--Tim May


