Date: Wed, 8 Sep 93 12:10:48 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Fever Swamp of "Rights"

Having only recently rejoined the list, I would never think of calling
for the end of some thread. Besides, with the new software....

But I can't help thinking the whole discussion of "rights" (of
Martians, of AIs, of cats, of deer, of vegetables, of fetuses, of ...)
is doomed. 

I take a more pragmatic view.

* some nonhumans I care for and may be said, loosely speaking, to have
a "contract," or at least a temporary bargain of some sort. My cats
expect to be fed and I expect them to behave themselves in my house.
It is certainly true that they are not verbal, may or may not be aware
of their own existence (in a way even remotely resembling our own
ability to consider our existence), and are not capable of signing
contracts. Still, I consider it a useful fiction that a deal has been
struck.

* I do not argue that my cats have "rights," only that I will
retaliate against anyone who agresses against them in certain ways.
That is , they have acquired "pseudo rights" by virtue of my
willingness to grant them and take action against anyone who violates
them.

* I think this situation is what applies to babies and small children
(and the severely retarded as well). They cannot negotiatie contracts,
in the sense Perry Metzger was arguing, and so cannot have "rights."
But the adults in their lives can extend them protection and thus give
them the same kind of pseudo rights mentioned above.

(I am uncomfortable with the term "rights," real or pseudo. Seems to
me we need a good term to cover this situation, a one-word term which
doesn't need a paragraph of explanation every time it is used. Any
ideas?)

* What of newborn babies and the whole abortion issue? (Is this still
off-limits for the list?) I often tell folks I'm "Pro Choice." When
they nod their heads, I hit 'em with the next part: "Yes, I support a
woman's right to infanticide."

(Actually, I take a pragmatic view. Nearly every culture which has
attempted to ban abortion has failed....too many natural and
artificial abortifacients exist for such a ban to be enforceable. But
most cultures which have banned infanticide--after some "christening
delay"--have been able to, as it is fairly obvious when a child is
born and then killed. So, unenforceable laws which require a
repressive state are very bad indeed, regardless of the abstract
arguments of whether fetuses have rights. And whether or not month-old
infants have rights or not (in may ways they're just like fetuses),
I'm willing--and so are most people, importantly--to accept a
situation in which they can't be killed at will, and that those who do
kill infants will be treated as murderers. I'm not saying these
infants have rights, just that they have the same kind of "pseudo
rights" mentioned above.)

* Maybe all "rights" are just these "pseudo rights," convenient
fictions agreed upon by many members of a group.

* I said the notion of "rights" is a fever swamp. Once the idea is
accepted, the swamp has been entered:

-a right to a good job
-a right to shelter
-a right to health care
-a right to an adequate diet
-a right not to be verbally abused
-a right not to have one's feelings hurt
-a right to declare more rights
...and so on, with no end


So, I find the "market approach," described in the PPL (privately
produced law) and anarchocapitalist literature (Freidman's "Machinery
of Freedom" of course) to be refreshingly shorn of the trappings of
"rights."

There are certainly things we will fight for, and protect, and these
may be seen as "pseudo rights" (e.g., my cats have a "right" to good
food and place in my lap), but in reality they are market decisions as
to what we will spend our money on, what steps we will take to defend
our property, and what encroachment by others we will accept. The
"Schelling point" argument, applied to moral choices.

This, I think, is a major step forward in the moral debate about the
role of government and the nature of ethics, and one which I hope
Extropians can teach to others.

-Tim May


