From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: Resources

Before I continue this discussion, I think I should explain a couple
things about my viewpoint regarding resources and, since the word has
been introduced, "environmentalism".

I do not believe that *energy* resources are being used up so fast as
to endanger human ability to develop new ones of equal or better
efficiency. It is my opinion that this particular "environmental" battle
is a smoke screen for deeper political agendas.

I do believe, however, that resources that humans have no way of
replacing may be being used up, irrevocably. I am speaking here
particularly of biological, environmental, and aesthetic resources.

I do believe that human intelligence and foresight can insure that such
resources are used efficiently and renew themselves indefinitely in
sufficiency to sustain human needs.

I do not believe that the preservation and protection of these resources
is something best left to the "state". In fact, I don't believe that
there's much of anything that's best left to a "state".

I believe that most "polluters" and resource "wasters" (and I admit the
subjectiveness of these terms) are only able to effectively compete in
the marketplace because they are protected by government bureaucracies
that, at best, hide their mistakes in an avalanche of paperwork, and,
at worst, subsidize them and enable them to remain in the marketplace
in defiance of the principles of open and enlightened trade.
 

Therefore, I believe that the best way to ensure the preservation of
these resources is to work for a free and enlightened marketplace. Hey,
I think the best solution to *most* human problems is a free and
enlightened marketplace.

I believe Environmentalism is Efficiency, and Efficiency is Profit.

Now in response to past threads:

James A. Donald writes:

In response to my example of Easter Island as a example of a
society passing a point of no return wrt resources:

>The social collapse on Easter Island was caused by a
>revolution, a revolution in which every member of the upper
>classes - the educated technology using classes, was killed
>in a horrible fashion.  Those who did not die in battle
>were burnt alive. Some people have speculated that the
>revolution was caused or worsened by resource exhaustion,
>but there is no concrete evidence of this.

Well regardless of what caused the revolution on Easter Island,
the fact remains that they managed to destroy certain resources
(apparently both human *and* material) and were unable to recover
from that loss. That was my point - humans *can* (if they try hard
enough and are ignorant enough), get themselves into a situation
where certain resources necessary to continue the "higher" functions
of civilization are just not available.


James continues on my comments re: Mayan collapse example:

>Again, this is speculation.  The immediate cause of
>collapse was the development of highly centralized and
>authoritarian states, which engaged in total war against
>each other, using totalitarian means to sustain these wars.
>Such states do not need resource exhaustion to collapse,
>although warfare over land undoubtedly contributed to the
>collapse.

The reasons for the Mayan collapse are of course speculation, as
is any history the observer didn't live through. But the historical
archeologists that I've read and heard speak on the matter seem
fairly convinced that a strong case can be made that the escalation
of Mayan warfare from the almost ritual to the absolute was a result
of continuing chronic resource shortages and that these shortages 

were brought on by poor infrastructure planning (and those decisions
were perhaps caused by religious/political shortsightedness - sounds
familiar). As the Mayan civilization aged, the Mayans built their
cities in the lowlands and pushed farms higher and further out, into
much less arable land. The inevitable shortages resulted in a new
phenomena (to the Mayans) - War for Resources. The genocidal warfare
and internal foment that followed apparently culminated in the complete
breakdown of their civilization.

I feel like I should go back to first sources to back up some of what
I'm saying with regards to Mayan history. I'll dig around in order to
confirm that the above broad outline of what happened is indeed current
theory.  


But I still feel that it's important to separate the ideas of resource
management and "environmentalism" from the particular political and social
agendas of some of those who might call themselves "environmentalists".

Environmentalism is, according to my Websters, advocacy of the preservation
or improvement of the natural environment; esp: the movement to control
pollution. Nothing here about how one achieves that preservation/improvement.
Of course, the easy way appears to be through more "state" involvement in
the affairs of the citizenry. That's certainly the method that the most
vocal "environmentalists" take. But we know better than that.

Many (most?) people, for whatever reasons, look to the "state" to insure
conditions favorable to their well-being. Just because they're wrong about
the ability of the "state" to do such, doesn't mean that the conditions
they hope to achieve or preserve are invalid. I believe that such is the
case with environmental concerns. Good idea - bad execution.

Cleaner water, soil and air *are* better for people. The beauty of
wilderness *is* a valuable resource in itself - a psychic and spiritual
resource if nothing else. And the genetic diversity of this planet's life
is a resource which we are only *just* beginning to appreciate.

And we are certainly capable of squandering them.

To use these resources inefficiently, to use them up completely, or to
reward others for doing so, is worse than immoral - it's bad business.

 -Jeff

