At 10:25 PM 9/7/93 -0700, Nick Szabo wrote:
>> This puts the entire onus of privacy on the principals. Why not do this for
>> everything, like theft and murder? 
>
>Theft and murder are coercion; the communication of knowledge is not.

So intellectual goods cannot be owned? This seems to preclude proscription
of libel, slander, blackmail, or copyright violation. I disagree.

Szabo's reply begs the question. He defines coercion as he likes, then says
one is coercion, the other is not. Part of the point is to decide what is
coercive. Do persons have rights to certain information about them, or
ownership of that information? Can such ownership be automatic (de facto),
without a previous explicit contract among all the parties? If so, why not
use legal enforcement to protect that ownership?

Miller seems to envision two alternative situations. In his preferred
world, the principals in a transaction can use their own means to keep
information about their transaction private, perhaps they could hire third
parties to protect that information, but they cannot use the law to protect
it. Once that information is known to someone not contractually obligated
to respect their secret, it can be published far and wide without legal
recourse.

In the alternative, even after information is discovered by the thief,
publishing/using the information would be illegal.

There are at least 4 criteria for deciding between these alternatives:

1) Introspection on the meaning of coercion.

2) Is enforcement cost-effective?

2') Is it easy for persons to know what is and isn't legal?

3) Which is preferred by the persons who will live under the resulting system?

Szabo is using 1, and I think Miller is as well, though not as obviously.
Miller says, "The fact that the transaction occurred is my information, and
I don't violate anyone's rights by reporting it further". This is proof by
emphatic assertion.

All four criteria will influence the outcome in a PPL system. I don't think
it is clear whether or not a PPL system would or should allow the
distribution of Lotus Marketplace, but I think Miller's reasons for
condemning its prohibition are weak. He could perhaps make a better case
for his alternative using 2'. But even then, it is not a decision between
liberty and tyranny.

