I write:
> >Theft and murder are coercion; the communication of knowledge is not.

David Burns responds:
> So intellectual goods cannot be owned? This seems to preclude proscription
> of libel, slander, blackmail, or copyright violation. I disagree.

Ask the practical question: which rights can be effectively enforced without
a police state?  Electronic libel and slander can be done with secure
anonymity, and violation of most electronic data copyrights is often
easy.  Same for electronic gossip.  Just as deer cannot punish the 
hunter for knocking off one of their own, so we cannot effectively 
enforce the law here.  Where the cost of enforcing a tort far outweighs 
the benefit of punishment, we _de facto_ have no rights of protection --
or, conversely, the other party obtains the right to act in a way
you'd rather he not (eg gossip).

This also ties in with the "nanarchy" debate.  The ever-shifting
tides of military and police weaponry, privacy technology, etc.
continually change the cost of enforcing laws.  On year we can
enforce some law; the next we can't, and so a "right" we thought
we had is lost, or a new right is enabled.  This phenomenon gets 
at the heart of why "nano-Gorts" cannot work in a society that retains 
technological progress.  For that matter, is also suggests that
our legal system and concept of "rights" have a built-in fluidity;
for example extrapoloating "free press" to include BBS's
(still in jeopardy, it seems) and the radio spectrum (which they decided 
in the early 20th century is not covered).

> Do persons have rights to certain information about them, or
> ownership of that information?

Let's put it this way: if you can (a) find out that the other
party has gossiped about you, (b) convince a judge of that, and (c) 
enforce the law without spending any tax dollars in the process, go for it.

The current law system, heavily subsidized by coerced third
parties, is not a good indicator of what kinds of tort 
enforcement are cost-effective.

